<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pune</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Poona</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pune, formerly Poona, is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan plateau</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> spent his youth there, and in the 18th century it became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> chief ministers, with their palace of Shaniwar Wada. The British took it in 1817 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-maratha-war">Third Anglo-Maratha War</a>. It later became a centre of education and social reform.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pune.jpg" alt="Pune" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ujjawal.Gayakwad · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pune_West_skyline_-_March_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pune lies on the western margin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a> at about 560 metres above sea level, on the leeward side of the Sahyadri range, and is about 149 kilometres by road south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>. The old city, with its many peths, stands on the east bank of the Mutha river, while the cantonment areas of Khadki and Camp were laid out by the British. The earliest reference to the town is a Rashtrakuta dynasty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plate of 937 CE, which names it Punya-vishaya. Copper plates of 858 and 868 CE show an agricultural settlement called Punnaka on the site, and the Pataleshwar Caves, a rock-cut temple complex, date from the same era.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Seuna Yadavas of Devagiri held Pune from the 9th century to 1327, and Muslim sultanates held it until the late 1600s. In 1599 the Ahmadnagar Sultanate granted it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a> to Maloji Bhosale, who passed it to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahaji-bhonsle">Shahaji</a>. Shahaji chose the town as the residence of his wife Jijabai and young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>, the future founder of the Maratha empire, and the Lal Mahal, where Shivaji spent his youth, was completed in 1640. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a>, appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> in 1720, moved his base to Pune in 1728 and began Shaniwar Wada, finished in 1730, which turned a kasbah into a city.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwas</a> were defeated at the Battle of Khadki on 5 November 1817 the British seized the city and placed it under the Bombay Presidency. They built a large military cantonment to the east, while the old city kept narrow lanes and districts divided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and religion. Pune became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> capital of the Governor of Bombay, and the Southern Command of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> was established there in 1895.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pune is the administrative headquarters of Pune district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, with 3,124,458 people in the city at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> is the official and most spoken language. The University of Pune, now Savitribai Phule Pune University, was founded in 1949, the National Chemical Laboratory in 1950 and the National Defence Academy in 1955. Among historic sites are Shaniwar Wada and the Aga Khan Palace, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> was interned during the Quit India movement. The first rules of modern badminton were drawn up in Pune in 1873.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pune” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 18th century real power passed from the Chhatrapati at Satara to the Peshwa's administration at <b>Pune</b>, which supervised a confederacy of semi-autonomous chiefs, among them the Gaekwads of Baroda, Holkars of Indore, Scindias of Gwalior and Bhonsles of Nagpur, who collected revenue and maintained armies while…</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Regional bodies such as the Indian Association of Calcutta, founded by Surendranath Banerjea in 1876, the <b>Poona</b> Sarvajanik Sabha and the Madras Mahajana Sabha prepared the ground for a national organisation.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1919 and 1966 scholars at the Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute, <b>Pune</b>, compared manuscripts from India and abroad and produced the Critical Edition, on 13,000 pages in 19 volumes, with the Harivamsha in two more volumes and six index volumes; it is the text usually used in Mahabharata…</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Mahatma Gandhi is said to have sung it over a hundred times while imprisoned at Yerawada Jail in <b>Pune</b> in the 1930s.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sare-jahan-se-accha">Sare Jahan se Accha</a></li><li><blockquote>Bhor merged into Bombay Province in 1948 and became part of Maharashtra in 1960, in <b>Pune</b> district.</blockquote><a href="/state/bhor-state">Bhor State</a></li></ul></section>
